{"pedagogical-reasoning": "The student is asking a methodological question about approach. I want to guide them toward the standard structured method (decision variables, objective, constraints, then Solver) rather than manual "cheapest lane" greedy assignment, since that's what the course teaches and what's needed for correctness with capacity/demand tradeoffs. I'll ask a Socratic question about why a greedy approach might fail here, to help them realize the need for the formal model.", "Student-facing-answer": "Good question. Think about it this way: with a greedy 'cheapest lane first' approach, could you run into a problem where a DC runs out of capacity before demand is met, forcing you to backtrack and reallocate?

Given that Allentown and Toledo each cap out at 100,000 lbs, and total demand across all four sites is fixed, do you think picking cheapest lanes one at a time will always land on the truly minimum-cost solution — or could it get stuck in a suboptimal allocation?

This is exactly why the course has you build the full spreadsheet model (decision variables for all 8 arcs, objective = sum of cost×flow, plus supply and demand constraints) and let Solver find the optimum, rather than hand-picking lanes. Give that a try — set up your 2×4 flow variables, and tell me what objective and constraints you come up with."}
